From 2462a4cf6ab17d357aaec5722092f4d28c4d8ec2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ariel Peretz <benoit.casse@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] smp: do not disable the BSP local APIC while starting the
 APs
To: bug-hurd@gnu.org

start_other_cpus() wrapped the whole AP bring-up window in
lapic_disable() / lapic_enable().  On the BSP that is both useless and
destructive:

  - useless: splhigh() is already in effect for the whole window, so the
    BSP takes no interrupt anyway (the APs were never covered by it,
    there is no execution on them yet);
  - destructive: clearing LAPIC_ENABLE makes the BSP unable to accept
    any IOAPIC interrupt.  The 100 Hz PIT edges that arrive while the
    APs come up are consumed without ever being delivered, and the master
    CPU never gets a clock tick again.

clock_interrupt() only advances time, the timeout list and last_hpc_read
on master_cpu, so the whole system clock then freezes: no time, no
timeout and no preemption.  rumpdisk then spins forever in its AHCI
attach, which is the "-smp 2 hangs at ahcisata0: 64-bit DMA" symptom
(measured with q35/kernel-irqchip=on: uptime frozen at 0.120 s,
elapsed_ticks at 12, while the guest is otherwise alive and keeps
polling the clock with IF=1).

The wedge is only cleared by a vCPU re-entry that forces the pending
interrupt to be re-injected (a gdb attach/detach, a pause), which is
why pausing the guest appeared to "unfreeze" it.

Measured after the fix on Debian GNU/Hurd under QEMU q35 -enable-kvm,
kernel built with --host=x86_64-pc-gnu --enable-ncpus=2: "-smp 2" goes
from 0/3 boots to 12/12 boots with nproc=2 and the clock at 100 Hz.
"-smp 1" is unaffected (3/3 boots).
---
 i386/i386/mp_desc.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/i386/i386/mp_desc.c b/i386/i386/mp_desc.c
index 1343861c..7ad205a3 100644
--- a/i386/i386/mp_desc.c
+++ b/i386/i386/mp_desc.c
@@ -301,16 +301,25 @@ start_other_cpus(void)
 
 	splhigh();
 
-	/* Disable IOAPIC interrupts (IPIs not affected).
-	 * Clearing this flag is similar to masking all
-	 * IOAPIC interrupts individually.
+	/* Do NOT disable the BSP's local APIC here.
 	 *
-	 * This is done to prevent IOAPIC interrupts from
-	 * interfering with SMP startup. splhigh() may be enough for BSP,
-	 * but I'm not sure.  We cannot control the lapic
-	 * on APs because we don't have execution on them yet.
+	 * splhigh() already blocks interrupts on the BSP for the whole AP
+	 * bring-up window, so masking the LAPIC buys nothing on the BSP
+	 * either.  It actively breaks it: clearing LAPIC_ENABLE makes the
+	 * BSP unable to accept *any* IOAPIC interrupt, so the 100 Hz PIT
+	 * edges that arrive while the APs come up are consumed without ever
+	 * being delivered, and the master CPU never gets a clock tick again.
+	 * Since clock_interrupt() only advances time, the timeout list and
+	 * last_hpc_read on master_cpu, the whole system clock then freezes:
+	 * no time, no timeout, no preemption, and rumpdisk spins forever in
+	 * its AHCI attach (measured: uptime stuck at 0.120 s, elapsed_ticks
+	 * at 12, while the guest is otherwise alive).  The wedge only clears
+	 * on a vCPU re-entry that forces the pending interrupt to be
+	 * re-injected (a gdb attach/detach, a vCPU pause), which is why a
+	 * pause appeared to "unfreeze" the guest.
+	 *
+	 * APs are left alone anyway: we have no execution on them yet.
 	 */
-	lapic_disable();
 
 	/* This is set once for all APs to use */
 	pmap_make_temporary_mapping();
@@ -337,8 +346,5 @@ start_other_cpus(void)
 	for (cpu = 1; cpu < ncpus; cpu++) {
 		interrupt_processor(cpu);
 	}
-
-	/* Re-enable IOAPIC interrupts as per setup */
-	lapic_enable();
 }
 #endif	/* NCPUS > 1 */
-- 
